course: dictionary
lesson: 355
title: Ante, the word that means before
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Ante is a word with more than one job. As a noun it is a stake, a bit of money put into the pot before a card game starts. As a prefix, ante- at the start of a word means before. «a.m. is Latin ante meridiem, before midday» shows this clearly: ante means before, and meridiem means midday.
explain: Once you know ante- means before, you can work out new words. Antenatal means before birth. Anteroom means a room before the main room. Each time, read the rest of the word and ask: before what? Then check your meaning in the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
explain: The phrase «up the ante» is different. To up the ante means to raise what is at stake, or make a situation more serious or demanding. The words around it give the clue: if the stakes go higher, things are getting more serious.
fact: Ante- at the start of a word means before.
fact: «a.m.» is Latin ante meridiem, before midday; «p.m.» is post meridiem, after midday.
fact: Antenatal means before birth.
fact: An ante is a stake put into the pot before a card game starts.
fact: To up the ante means to increase what is at stake or make a situation more serious.
exercise: «To say which half you mean, people add "am" (before midday, from Latin "ante meridiem")». What does ante mean in ante meridiem? | After | Inside | Midday | *Before
exercise: Ante- at the start of a word usually means what? | Under | *Before | After | Around
exercise: Jo says, «The stakes just went up, so now we are upping the ante.» What does upping the ante mean? | Putting the cards away and going home | Eating a snack | *Raising what is at stake, making things more serious | Winning the game straight away
test: «The guests waited in the anteroom before the party.» What does ante- mean in anteroom? | After | Around | Inside | *Before
test: What is an ante in a card game? | A prize for the winner | *A stake put into the pot before the game starts | A rule about who goes first | The cards dealt at the start
exam: What does a.m. mean in «The a.m. train leaves at seven»? | Midnight | After midday | *Before midday | Late in the afternoon
